Each episode in this series will explore one crucial metric and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

10
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:02,640
With me today, Jackson Shuttleworth, Group Product Manager, Retention Team Lead at Duolingo.

11
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:16,640
On the podcast, I talk with Jackson about Duolingo's data driven approach to retention, how social connections dramatically improve reactivation, and why letting users set their preferred notification time backfires.

12
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:19,680
Hey Jackson, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

13
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,440
Yeah, thank you for having me, dude.

14
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:27,520
So I'm really looking forward to talking about reactivation and keeping users in the app and getting them back when they've left.

15
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:30,000
But for context, Dualingo is such a huge company.

16
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:34,560
I did want to give listeners just a little insight into what you work on day to day.

17
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,600
So Dualingo, I manage our retention efforts.

18
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:40,640
This sits within the growth or the growth org at Duolingo.

19
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,040
So this is focusing on how do we grow our user base, specifically focusing on DAUs.

20
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:50,880
And so within retention, a lot of this is focusing on the most habit forming features in our app.

21
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:53,600
So you've probably heard of the Duolingo streak.

22
00:01:53,840 --> 00:02:06,800
This is what I spend a lot of time every day thinking about how to better optimize with some of the other teams under my umbrella, focusing on things like notifications, push notifications, emails, things that are both helping keep the user in the app.

23
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,920
And that's what we focus most on is how do we get you to never churn.

24
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,800
But then once we do churn, our wants to use your app.

25
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,840
Or once the user does churn, how do we get them back into the app as quickly as possible?

26
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,720
Because you learn that the longer the user has been away, the harder it is to get them to come home.

27
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:24,800
So in the report, we're not going to share usage metrics.

28
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:30,000
We are going to be sharing subscriber metrics and subscriber reactivation metrics.

29
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:37,840
But what's going to be fun chatting with you is that you're not as focused on the subscriber reactivation, but you're focused on the user reactivation.

30
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:42,560
And ultimately, that's upstream of the subscriber reactivation.

31
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,520
If you ever have a hope to win someone back to your system.

32
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:49,680
So let's chat about that.

33
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:54,160
Like, what's your philosophy around how to get people back into the app?

34
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,800
The easiest way to retain a user is to not let them start to begin with.

35
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:15,680
And so a lot of the features that you'll see on Duolingo are really focused on building that daily habit, even and we've run so many experiments on our streak, even things that have users spending one, two days away from the app make it significantly more difficult to get them to come back in two or three days later.

36
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:22,160
So a lot of our focus is getting users, if they can use the app every day to use it every day.

37
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:26,720
I will say that again, this team does manage things like notifications, emails.

38
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,160
This is a lot of our power.

39
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:38,800
Users have taken a few days off getting them back in the app, and we send a series of seven notifications if you for each day that you don't use the app.

40
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,760
And so you take a day off, we send you notification.

41
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:43,760
Second day off, we send you notification.

42
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:51,440
At seven days, we turn off our practice reminder notifications, which are our most powerful notification because the additional.

43
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:57,440
value we get of continuing to reach out to you after seven days is significantly lower.

44
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,120
I mean, honestly, even by day seven, the efficacy has, has dropped significantly.

45
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:07,040
And so that is sort of an insight into you can't wait till day seven to get that user to come back into the app.

46
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:10,480
You probably can't wait till day five if they're not coming back by day three or four.

47
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:13,520
Again, depending on the use case for us, that's for a daily use app.

48
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:14,560
That's definitely the case.

49
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:24,240
Man, it is just going to be tough sledding to get them to come back for a lot of apps that aren't as focused on daily use, which I think is a good portion.

50
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:36,480
of the people who will be listening is a golf app where you're going to go golfing two or three times a month or even a fitness app, which maybe you're doing really well to be working out three times a week.

51
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:45,120
For the people who have passed that seven day mark, who are more challenging, I feel like maybe that's more relatable to a lot of the people who are going to be listening to this.

52
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:50,000
How do you think about those people who have been out of the app for seven days, who are that kind of harder problem?

53
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:53,120
We think about it across a few different time scales.

54
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:57,040
I'll say the first one is the first few weeks after we've lost you.

55
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:00,960
So we have a progress report type email that goes out to users.

56
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:16,000
The fact that it's an email is nice because it gives us significantly more space for us to highlight elements of your progress report versus just a push notification where we're really having to rely heavily on you tapping into this to go back to an app that maybe you've installed.

57
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:18,000
So here actually channel is important.

58
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:26,160
And so this is nice because it's a weekly progress comparison where week to week we say, hey, how did you do this week versus how did you do last week?

59
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:30,160
If you've taken a week or two off, obviously that's framed.

60
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:43,680
somewhat negatively, but what you could do is, and there's all sorts of different copy that we've tested, there's ways to draw attention to, like, hey, have you taken a break, come back and jump in, or hey, man, it seems like a slow week, how much are you going to lend this week?

61
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:52,080
There's different ways to make that copy and messaging to users either a little bit passive aggressive or hopeful or like a challenge.

62
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:53,280
And this is really important.

63
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:56,400
What I'd say is like you got to test all these different copy variants.

64
00:05:56,560 --> 00:06:02,560
One of the things that doolingo is we test so much copy, like a disgusting amount of copy, particularly with a lot of our notification type work.

65
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,760
And different users are different and you can have as many hypotheses as you want about what works.

66
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:08,960
You just have to test it.

67
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,840
A lot of it is just engagement metrics that we're showing you.

68
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:13,040
How much XP did you earn?

69
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,400
How much time did you spend in there?

70
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:24,560
And some of that's because it's more complex for us to show you more like, hey, you learned this word or, you know, like the feasibility of that is higher as we think about ROI.

71
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:49,120
What we've realized is that like just showing a user how much they've done and allowing them to infer and using copy to support it, like, hey, look at all the stuff you've done, it's much easier and honestly gets, you know, a lot of the bang for the buck for Duolingo., where you're obviously using Duolingo to learn a language, make better use of your free time, it's easier for us to tell this narrative of like you use Duolingo, but isn't that obviously a good thing, this time you spent.

72
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:57,280
But I think, you know, there's a lot that you can do as for me, the product manager, to just draw attention to, here's the activity in the app.

73
00:06:57,680 --> 00:06:59,120
It is necessarily a good thing.

74
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,200
Don't you want to come back and get more of that?

75
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:02,400
You can frame data.

76
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:06,080
Lots of different ways to make it seem motivating.

77
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:08,880
And I would challenge your listeners to like really.

78
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:11,760
really look out because again, these users get churn.

79
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
If they're not compelled by your message, they're not going to come back.

80
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:17,040
So then moving on to the longer time span.

81
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:22,560
So I guess it was like the seven to thirty days and then like after thirty days is I guess the next bunch.

82
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:23,440
Yeah, yeah.

83
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:25,280
So a few different things I'll say.

84
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:39,680
One thing that's that's hot on our mind is we do a big year in review type similar to like a Spotify wrapped where at the end of the year we're showing you stats and information about your learning and it's super fun.

85
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:44,480
I recommend anyone who's been on on Duolingo this year, like, go check it out.

86
00:07:44,880 --> 00:08:00,240
Even if you were a user who used it for a few days in April, we can still show you something and give you value and give you value pretty much immediately after reopening the app that is awesome and exciting and makes you laugh and makes you think like, oh, actually, hey, I could use that app again.

87
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:11,440
And so I think the more you can frame some of these outreach moments, even months later, in a way that when the user comes back, they're getting something delightful.

88
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:13,200
They're getting a little endorphine rush.

89
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:13,920
It is something fun.

90
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,120
And particularly if you can tie it to.

91
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:31,680
them again, the user that's used the app for a few days in April is not going to have a lot of really high value analysis we can give them, but we could still make it a super delightful experience and that might be what it takes to get them over the bump and using the app again.

92
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:47,600
And so I think the more you can tie it to tie these outreach moments to either specific times of the year, specific information, what can you deliver to the user in terms of value and not just like, here's a product update, much better performance though when we can actually attach it to something.

93
00:08:49,360 --> 00:09:00,320
Speaking of performance, are there any campaigns that stick out in your mind as especially effective, like leading up to this summer after COVID, we said, hey, are you travelling this summer?

94
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:11,760
Was there any like just knocked it out of the park kind of thing that you recall specific campaigns less so then there are specific times of year that are always really, really important to us.

95
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:19,360
And it's exactly as you said, none of this should probably be all too surprising because we try to build our campaigns around the mental model that users have around using our product.

96
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:22,080
So like back to school., always very big.

97
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:23,360
People are going back to school.

98
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:24,880
You want to start learning again.

99
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:29,200
Even like people who are not in school, you kind of get to that mindset of like going to class.

100
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,280
And so that's a really big time for us.

101
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:33,840
We make sure to target that with messaging that resonates.

102
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,080
Same thing, beginning of the year, New Year's resolutions.

103
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:39,920
We are Dualingos often learning language, learning anything.

104
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:42,000
Definitely a New Year's resolution that folks have.

105
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:48,320
And it's funny because that actually works quite well with our year to review type program where we're getting users back at the end of the year.

106
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:54,640
Hopefully some of those users are staying around at the beginning of the year where then they're making that commitment to, hey, I'm going to to learn a language.

107
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:55,680
I'm back on Duolingo.

108
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:56,560
I'm just going to keep it up.

109
00:09:56,800 --> 00:10:04,000
But again, for us, it's less the actual, for me, anyway, less the actual creative and more, how are we targeting the right times of the year with the right message?

110
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:08,560
How do you think about data and experimentation in relation to all of this?

111
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:11,600
I know, I mean, Duolingo famously does a ton of testing.

112
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:20,400
So when you're thinking about these reactivating users and winning users back, how do you think about it in terms of data and making it more data informed?

113
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:29,120
For Duolingo notifications, again, be it email or push, are a critical part of our features set and a critical driver of growth for us.

114
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:34,960
We have machine learning driven systems that choose the right notification copy for the right user.

115
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:36,560
They optimize on their own.

116
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:38,400
I mean, for a PM, it's really nice.

117
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:41,920
You just kind of like stick copy variants in there with AI.

118
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,600
We'll probably not even have to do that soon enough.

119
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:47,040
Like, yeah, I will stick and then it optimizes, which is great.

120
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:54,080
And if once you have the keys, you know, or the size and scale to do that, building a system like that is awesome.

121
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:56,480
Never sleep on copy.

122
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:07,280
And with notifications, it is like infinitely easy to cop constantly be testing because all it can rely on is that subject line and maybe some preview copy, your push notification.

123
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:09,920
All it can rely on is what's in the title and what's in the body.

124
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:15,840
You don't have the whole feature set that it's supporting it or the fact that the user is in the app already.

125
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:17,680
Like, it's just all copy.

126
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:18,560
It's all testing.

127
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:24,800
And so again, depends on the size of your population, but you can look at open, click through open rates, open rates or click through rates.

128
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:26,480
Again, be it email or notification.

129
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:29,680
I mean, you can get, I would guess, statistic data pretty quickly.

130
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:36,080
But again, I just see a lot of companies, oh, I don't know, looking at notifications, it's clear that there's some that are just like, Okay, cool.

131
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:36,960
This notification worked.

132
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:37,680
Let's move on.

133
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:45,280
I mean, our practice reminder notification, we've been experimenting on that, experimenting on that notification for six years for a VM that loves experimentation.

134
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:46,480
It's beautiful to see.

135
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:50,960
So really honing that message, because again, I said this earlier, these users are churned.

136
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,280
You don't know what they remember about their experience.

137
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:55,920
There's a lot that you don't know about this user.

138
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:04,880
This is the user you need to experiment on almost more than any other because it's just less likely that you have a good handle on what it is that they need.

139
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:07,120
Like all of our scheduling is smart.

140
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:18,720
Although one of the funny things about our scheduling that we realized is that I think there's this desire from companies when you start, when you onboard and you say, hey, when do you want us to remind you?

141
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:21,280
In the morning, in the evening, at this time.

142
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:32,160
What we realized is that, you know, if as soon as you set a time, the likelihood that you were going to be free at that time for perpetuity, which is what a lot of apps assume, is just zero.

143
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:33,680
Like, you all have busy lives.

144
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,680
We all use these apps at different times of the day.

145
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:38,800
And what was always a winner for us was scheduling.

146
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:45,600
the next day's notification for 23.5 hours after you practiced for the first time that day.

147
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,920
And so we basically just were always targeting, when did you practice the day before?

148
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:50,640
The next day.

149
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:59,360
So there is a lot of, and this doesn't seem smart, although we've tested a lot of different strategies here, and this is the one that continues to win.

150
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:02,400
Any other top tips for getting people back?

151
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:10,480
One that's been an interesting thread for us to pull on is social in the app as it pertains to win backs.

152
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:15,120
Dualingo has been investing in our app social network now for many years.

153
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:25,120
I think we're at like 55 or 60 percent of our users have a friend on the app and these aren't all like my mom for instance uses Dualingo.

154
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:26,160
We're friends in the app.

155
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:27,840
Oh, that's a great win back man.

156
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,440
A mama in the app like, hey, Jackson.

157
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:32,000
Yeah.

158
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:35,680
Well, whenever my mom lives in the street, it cuts particularly deep.

159
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:42,080
So one of the things that we've again threads we've been pulling on is getting users to resurrect each other.

160
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:44,320
So if you have a friend who's been gone for.

161
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:49,200
for months and months, prompting the user to hey, get them to come back.

162
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:57,680
And this is one of our best performing resurrection campaigns or win back campaigns just because A, it feels more personal.

163
00:13:58,160 --> 00:14:01,360
It's not Duolingo saying, hey, David, come back.

164
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:04,080
It's Jackson saying, hey, come back and learn with me.

165
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:07,760
And then what's cool is now we have other features in the app.

166
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:10,560
So one feature we've recently launched is Friend Streaks.

167
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:12,560
It's a way for you to learn with someone else.

168
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:24,080
Now we have a social feature set that when you can get this person back into the app now that there's been a personal appeal made, you can ease them into a more social experience with that person.

169
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:30,080
So it's a nice way to pair the out of app communication with the in app experience.

170
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:40,960
But even for folks that it's like, again, the random person that you find on your Learboard, still a much higher click through rate for these people that you've never met in real life.

171
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:45,040
Even messages, winback messages from those folks still perform better.

172
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:47,440
As we're wrapping up, anything else we wanted to share?

173
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:47,920
Yes.

174
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:50,640
If any of this sounds interesting for you to work on.

175
00:14:51,440 --> 00:15:01,200
We are hiring actually we're hiring both for my team looking for a senior to lead PM who wants to work in Pittsburgh helping drive a lot of our retention efforts.

176
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:04,480
So again, the streak excites you to do that professionally.

177
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:07,440
And then the other area is we're starting to build a notifications team.

178
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:18,640
So looking for a senior to lead PM in New York who wants us to help build out, you know, an even smarter, more robust and thoughtful notifications platform.

179
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:22,320
So this is a very relevant discussion for us today.

180
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:23,520
And if you find it relevant.

181
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:26,240
to what you like to think about, definitely reach out.

182
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:26,800
Awesome.

183
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:28,400
Well, thank you so much for joining me today.

184
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:29,520
It was so insightful.

185
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:30,640
A lot of really fun stuff.

